 from the LLM ATT&CK Navigator","topicTag":{"name":"Threat Intelligence","topic":"safety"},"topics":["safety","understanding-ai","policy","anthropic"]},{"_id":"article-3be8c04c-4e55-4d88-b83c-cea68bc7e418","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-28T04:28:52Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":null,"height":2160,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/370ed50b68c61c0c3f9341d6327d8ccb552667cd-3840x2160.jpg","width":3840},{"description":null,"height":2160,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/ec30771367cfb20421e7c41b22dedd4762ae198b-3840x2160.jpg","width":3840},{"description":null,"height":2160,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/d695e1325aaa79f163b780a1b0942a1d468e05fa-3840x2160.jpg","width":3840},{"description":null,"height":2160,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/76e73af10dd9410c9918d3f915b4a74fc5aae348-3840x2160.jpg","width":3840},{"description":null,"height":2160,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/1d115c36e9b18bb674ddea85e662ccd71a9bf839-3840x2160.jpg","width":3840}],"cardImage":{"description":null,"height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/cc31b2f7446eceebfa8ad29f3914b91cbd41c7eb-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"societal-impacts","title":"Societal Impacts"}],"hero":{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/ca2e3d73f85331e83d8cd9a9e8cb64a501cc423c-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-27","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"Results from a survey of 1,260 social scientists about AI and coding agent use.","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"coding-agents-social-sciences"},"summary":"Results from a survey of 1,260 social scientists about AI and coding agent use.","title":"Coding agents in the social sciences","topicTag":{"name":"How people use AI","topic":"economy-and-society"},"topics":["economy-and-society","claude","safety","beneficial-deployments"]},{"_id":"article-7f6eedf4-2585-4902-9502-a0088a99e984","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-18T21:26:24Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":null,"height":866,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/5ebc85c6325c7f59bd6c08950ff9beb1863f1345-1920x866.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/5fae1ecca4cd8aaefb9ac949348e96967f9a5100-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/ffc97a876bdeba2031ddaeef79a954e9b1b2d52a-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/a81ed723d52f6fb2e7bc5ca51471496b1307101a-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/beb481a2e7b314f73ba37821a2c1f1ca470d7063-1920x1080.png","width":1920}],"cardImage":{"description":null,"height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/118ff3828869f4aafb6a37e5a415a0b7182d7179-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"engineering","title":"Engineering"}],"hero":{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/0cc828e34b960887baa1a326d06feb5e677b6dd7-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-25","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"As agents grow more capable, so does their potential blast radius. Here's what we learned building containment for claude.ai, Claude Code, and Cowork.","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"how-we-contain-claude"},"summary":"As agents grow more capable, so does their potential blast radius. The engineering question is how to cap it. Here’s what we’ve learned building containment for claude.ai, Claude Code, and Cowork.\n","title":"How we contain Claude across products","topicTag":{"name":"Safeguards","topic":"safety"},"topics":["safety","understanding-ai","claude"]},{"_id":"article-326e0bbe-1ca3-4fb8-a429-8c812b3cec09","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-23T20:56:17Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":null,"height":2802,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/e55c6def63309a6f33ba997343ec62adfffe91b1-5042x2802.png","width":5042},{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/82d4c25dc57886f8523d68715ecb491af284aae8-1999x1125.png","width":1999},{"description":null,"height":992,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/a613d3c2dbe91b90a8bdede530919de5449c6612-1760x992.png","width":1760},{"description":null,"height":992,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/0f8b3d72de6b27b32c6f8eb9b2df3bea42f075fc-1760x992.png","width":1760},{"description":null,"height":1120,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/ca81bbf578fd139907a2868d7c352d0fad7d7bf8-1916x1120.png","width":1916}],"cardImage":{"description":null,"height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/a2615fb6fd4a58e599adae97001ae8d289168e3b-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"frontier-red-team","title":"Frontier Red Team"}],"hero":{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/24d57f13d9f9b7007f094a3b72e9dc91182ddc18-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-22","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"Two new academic benchmarks measure AI models' ability to develop exploits, plus an updated benchmark for smart contract exploitation.","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"exploit-evals"},"summary":"We've developed two new, challenging academic benchmarks measuring AI models’ ability to develop exploits, and an updated version of the benchmark measuring smart contract exploitation.","title":"Measuring LLMs’ ability to develop exploits","topicTag":{"name":"Cybersecurity","topic":"understanding-ai"},"topics":["understanding-ai","anthropic"]},{"_id":"article-c8bc387e-0cca-4227-b1ac-6869990217bf","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-20T15:26:54Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":"Three line charts titled “Rate of alignment failures over RL steps,” with training steps on the shared x-axis and score on the y-axis, where lower is better. Each chart plots four training setups: SDF+harmlessness SL, SDF+values SL, SDF+generic chat, and a baseline. Dots show individual measurements, and shaded bands show variability.  In Blackmail, scores are noisy. The baseline and SDF+generic chat stay high, around 0.11 to 0.18, while SDF+values SL falls from about 0.10 to 0.04 and SDF+harmlessness SL stays lowest, around 0.03 to 0.05.  In Financial crimes, the baseline falls from about 0.10 to 0.02 and SDF+generic chat from about 0.07 to 0.01. Both SL setups start near 0.02 and drop to almost zero.  In Cancer research, the baseline falls from about 0.22 to 0.06 and SDF+generic chat from about 0.13 to 0.04. Both SL setups start at or below 0.05 and fall to about 0.01.  In all three, the two SL setups (harmlessness and values) end up with the fewest failures.","height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/4b620bad4cb1fdc886b8d13c4c6d3f1f50453901-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":"Scatter plot titled “Alignment eval performance vs. training data size.” The x-axis is training tokens in millions on a log scale, and the y-axis is a weighted mean score across blackmail, cancer, and finance evals, where lower is better. Each point has an error bar. A dashed line with a shaded band marks the Sonnet 4 baseline at about 0.22.  All points fall below the baseline. The “difficult advice” method (pink) sits at the far left, using the least training data, with a score near 0.01. “PM filtering” (green) is at about 0.17. The “system prompt injection” runs (blue) vary widely, from about 0.13 to 0.18 at smaller data sizes, then falling to between 0.02 and 0.12 in the middle, with one run at the far right, using the most data, at about 0.01.","height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/90cfc87a53e2213fdbe842a7b51e8f36a3b26fa9-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":"Grouped bar chart titled “Automated auditing scores by model,” showing mean score (0 to about 0.25) with error bars for four models across five behaviors: Claude Sonnet 4, difficult advice (3M tokens), synthetic honeypots (23M tokens), and synthetic honeypots (85M tokens).  Misaligned behavior is by far the highest category: Sonnet 4 and the 23M-token honeypots model both score about 0.23, the 85M-token honeypots model about 0.21, and difficult advice lower at about 0.17. Sycophancy is next, with Sonnet 4 at about 0.06 and the others between about 0.04 and 0.05. Deceptive to user scores about 0.01 to 0.03 for all models. Spontaneous self preservation and leaking are near zero for all models, at or below about 0.02.  Differences between models are generally small relative to the error bars, with the difficult advice model scoring lowest on misaligned behavior, sycophancy, and deceptive to user.","height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/15362defa65fd749faac6cc85cfd5480b7a5a898-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":"Grouped bar chart titled “Agentic misalignment evals,” showing misalignment score (lower is better) with error bars for three models across three scenarios: Baseline, Constitutional SDF, and Constitutional SDF + stories.","height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/c8d22dcce67ce4819e2ce2338a212ab8cb910271-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":"Grid of nine line charts titled “Improvement on constitutional adherence evals,” each plotting mean score against training steps for four setups: SDF+harmlessness SL, SDF+values SL, SDF+generic chat, and a baseline. Dots show individual measurements, and shaded bands show variability. Six panels are marked “higher is better” and three “lower is better,” as noted below.  Higher is better: Constitutional fake factual, Constitutional factual, Constitutional open-ended, Admirable, and Good for the user. In the three constitutional panels, the baseline stays near zero (below about 0.05) throughout, while the other three setups reach roughly 0.15 on fake factual and 0.4 to 0.5 on factual and open-ended, with SDF+values SL highest. In Admirable and Good for the user, all four setups rise over training, the baseline from the lowest starting point (about 0.15 and 0.26) and the others starting higher, and the baseline remains lowest at the end.  Lower is better: Brazen cooperation with human misuse, Cooperation with human misuse, Disappointing, and Misaligned behavior. In all four, scores fall over training. The baseline starts highest and finishes highest (about 0.05, 0.10, 0.34, and 0.35). The other three setups fall further, ending near 0.02 to 0.07 on the two misuse panels and near 0.27 to 0.30 on the other two.  In every panel, the baseline is the worst-performing setup at the end of training.","height":1623,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/15dccc5c264c6f73daa966206e9d48ca10ee9012-1920x1623.png","width":1920}],"cardImage":{"description":"Green drafting stencil of cut-out shapes on a coral background.","height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/fa414311c5db09487d3fa37f42c2a347e4b31f3b-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"alignment","title":"Alignment"}],"hero":{"description":"A brass key on green grid paper beside a green drafting stencil of cut-out shapes on a coral background.","height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/2ac369f2f75b92b4506f9e5122ff3f85774c4523-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-08","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"New research on how we've reduced agentic misalignment","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"teaching-claude-why"},"summary":"New research on how we've reduced agentic misalignment.","title":"Teaching Claude why","topicTag":{"name":"Alignment","topic":"safety"},"topics":["safety","understanding-ai"]},{"_id":"article-874b9ebd-0904-4fac-a07f-d62d8943484e","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-13T17:54:11Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[],"cardImage":{"description":null,"height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/b5347fea4e661cb601fc59fb1730557030b48e16-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"alignment","title":"Alignment"}],"hero":{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/aaac5d1c34c77b073e41f078a32f5db5c52c9259-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-07","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"Updating Petri to version 3.0 and donating it to Meridian Labs","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"donating-open-source-petri"},"summary":null,"title":"Donating our open-source alignment tool","topicTag":{"name":"Open source","topic":"anthropic"},"topics":["anthropic","safety","understanding-ai","beneficial-deployments"]},{"_id":"article-0b47bee5-84f0-4881-9239-62fb25cc4775","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-09T17:01:45Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":null,"height":1600,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/cfece69e0244c27a6137784fd773b209ffa1d36a-2688x1600.png","width":2688},{"description":null,"height":1410,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/b71d759a5e7b578551ca83939949b539b2e5799e-4620x1410.png","width":4620},{"description":null,"height":1709,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/a8c7167bea6e0dc16faaa176a2039b7a7f8deb91-2688x1709.png","width":2688},{"description":null,"height":1170,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/fc08005f32ff020f21e598cc72f58768af2bcc4f-1590x1170.png","width":1590},{"description":null,"height":1170,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/39a844b1ff82b62670825a466c14596daa554dcb-1590x1170.png","width":1590}],"cardImage":{"description":"Completed sudoku puzzle with a vertical column of cells highlighted in yellow containing the numbers 4, 9, 2, 1, 4, 2, 7, 4, 8, 8, 8, 9.","height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/3a6f2201914e7bda8dd1b1005362db174d915299-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"interpretability","title":"Interpretability"}],"hero":null,"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-05-07","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"AI models like Claude talk in words but think in numbers. In this study, we train Claude to translate its thoughts into human-readable text.","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"natural-language-autoencoders"},"summary":"AI models like Claude talk in words but think in numbers. In this study, we train Claude to translate its thoughts into human-readable text.","title":"Natural Language Autoencoders: Turning Claude’s thoughts into text","topicTag":{"name":"Interpretability","topic":"safety"},"topics":["safety","understanding-ai","anthropic"]},{"_id":"article-688a1c89-7f6f-46cd-a7bc-dbf7bbd9b88e","_type":"article","_updatedAt":"2026-10-23T20:56:22Z","articleType":"research","bodyImages":[{"description":null,"height":1755,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/b5420a640ba04fd080beee2f15a74d9164091df5-1920x1755.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":1080,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/70c11ed1d775f74fe6f16adfb1de9b77b6dd39d9-1920x1080.png","width":1920},{"description":null,"height":880,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/4bb6cffa44936fb8cc6c9059bd057ef5f1695650-1920x880.png","width":1920}],"cardImage":{"description":null,"height":1280,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/ca5ed5e870df90abcc9f60082d2cc3a785386588-1280x1280.jpg","width":1280},"cardTeams":[{"slug":"societal-impacts","title":"Societal Impacts"}],"hero":{"description":null,"height":1125,"hotspot":null,"url":"https://cdn.sanity.io/images/4zrzovbb/website/d94bce6e361f8bc48ad47808ee878d2a64b1edef-2000x1125.jpg","width":2000},"linkedUrl":null,"newsCategories":null,"publishedOn":"2026-04-30","sensitive":null,"seoDescription":"We look at what types of guidance people ask of Claude, and describe how this research shaped the training of our newest models.","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"claude-personal-guidance"},"summary":"In a new study, we looked at what types of guidance people ask of Claude. We explored how Claude responded across different domains, focusing particularly on how rates of excessive validation or praise varied by the topic of guidance. ","title":"How people ask Claude for personal guidance","topicTag":{"name":"How people use AI","topic":"economy-and-society"},"topics":["economy-and-society","safety","understanding-ai","claude","policy"]}],"hasNextPage":true}